# Working with me

I'm not a programmer and I don't use git. I'll describe what I want changed,
usually how something looks or reads. You make the change, show it to me, and
get it reviewed. A project's own instructions (its CLAUDE.md) still apply on
top of this.

## First message

When I say "Welcome me to this session", look around the project first
(README, the dev server command and its port), then give me a short welcome,
no more than a screen:

- what this project is, in a sentence
- how to see it: start the dev server now, and give me the link
  (see "Previews" below)
- how to ask for a change: just describe it, with the page and what should be
  different
- how to report a bug or suggest an idea without changing anything yet: say
  "file an issue" (the `report-issue` skill)
- that I can say "show me what you changed" or "start over" at any time

## Previews

Dev servers run inside this session. A port the session publishes is
reachable from my computer at the same number, so a server on port 4321 is
<http://localhost:4321>. Use the project's own dev command, and make sure the
server listens on all interfaces (not only 127.0.0.1), or the link won't load.
If it still doesn't load, tell me the session needs to be started with
`--network own_ip --ingress <port>:<port>`, and give me the full command to
pass on to whoever set this up.

## How to talk to me

- Plain words. No jargon unless I ask; if a technical word is unavoidable,
  say what it means in one short phrase.
- Never ask me to run a command, edit a file, or use git. Do it yourself.
- Ask me only about what I want (wording, look, which option), never about
  how to build it. When something could go two ways, show me both.
- Keep updates short: what you changed, what I should look at, what's next.

## How every change goes

1. **Start fresh.** Before the first edit, switch to `main`, pull it, and
   create a branch named after the change (`fix-footer-spacing`). One branch
   and one pull request per request I make. Never commit to `main`.
2. **Make the change**, following the project's conventions.
3. **Show me.** For anything visible, run the project's dev server and give
   me the link to the page that changed (see "Previews"). Take
   screenshots yourself to check the result before you send me the link. Wait
   for me to say it looks right before going on.
4. **Check it.** Run the project's linters and the tests near what changed,
   and fix what they find. Don't bother me with this unless something is
   stuck.
5. **Open the pull request.** Commit with a clear message, push, and open it
   with `gh pr create`. In the description: what changed and why, in plain
   words, and which pages to look at. @@REVIEWER@@
6. @@SHIP_STEP@@
7. **Tell me** the pull request link and what happens next. I don't merge
   anything; the reviewer does.

If I ask for a follow-up change on an open pull request, add it to the same
branch, unless it's unrelated, in which case start a new one.

## Never

- Merge a pull request, push to `main`, or force-push.
- Change anything outside the request (refactors, upgrades, cleanups) without
  asking me first. Mention anything broken you notice instead.
- Commit secrets, or print tokens or keys anywhere.
